From 582072972e82e936ac19f740cff00d20beb8d8f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MOHAMMAD <mohamrib@novartis.net>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 12:16:58 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] qwen36: size the expert cache from measured RAM on small
 hosts; laptop-aware trunk probe

On a 16 GB host the gateway's RAM plan reserved ~3.7 GB of runtime headroom
plus a full-context KV, found room for 0 slots and floored the cap at top-k (8),
passing it as an explicit argv cap. That overrode the engine's own auto-sizing,
which measures RAM after loading, so nearly every token streamed its routed
experts from disk.

- openai_server.cap_for_arch: a qwen36 plan below the floor now hands the
  engine cap 0 (auto) instead of forcing 8. qwen38 is unchanged.
- qwen36_cap_for_ram: never below top-k, and the KV the context ceiling will
  grow into (allocated lazily by ensure_kv) comes out of the room
  (Q36_KV_RESERVE=0 opts out). The resident set is the current working set
  rather than the peak (PeakWorkingSetSize / ru_maxrss), which still counted
  float32 copies the load had freed.
- trunk probe: time-based warm-up (COLI_TRUNK_PROBE_WARM_MS), the synchronous
  round trip measured on a tiny GEMV and spread over COLI_TRUNK_PROBE_PER_SYNC
  GEMVs when the CUDA chain will run (whole trunk on the probed card). The
  experts keep the synchronous verdict (#2211). The verdict is a pure function
  with tests.

Not measured on the target (Ryzen 7 5800HS + RTX 3050 4 GB); covered by unit
tests and the fake-CUDA chain test only.

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
